{"seq": 12, "type": "assistant_message", "ts": "2026-11-22T16:26:43.986481+00:00", "payload": {"reasoning": "Good. Module set up. Now write the main program.\n\nThe approach: each node generates tickets of the form \"<nodeID>-<random-session-token>-<counter>\" or simply a UUID-like. Since uniqueness is checked across the whole test, and node IDs are distinct, nodeID + counter is sufficient. But to be extra safe against a restart of a process (Maelstrom may restart nodes? The nemesis is only partition, but the workload `--availability total` also... there might be process crashes if nemesis includes them — no, only partition), I'll include a random session token too.\n\nActually, another consideration: the tickets should be unique, but also the checker for unique-ids workload in Maelstrom just collects all `id` values and checks for duplicates. Any JSON value works. Strings with node ID prefix are fine.\n\nHmm, but wait — is the grading possibly more than Maelstrom's checker? \"The attempt passes only if it builds and every run is valid.\" — valid means maelstrom reports :valid? true. The unique-ids checker checks uniqueness only (plus availability requirement from --availability total which checks that every request got a response).\n\nSo the simplest approach works. Let me write it.\n\nAlso note: rate 1000/s over 5 nodes, 30s -> 30000 tickets. Trivial.\n\nLet me write main.go:\n\n```go\npackage main\n\nimport (\n\t\"crypto/rand\"\n\t\"encoding/hex\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"sync/atomic\"\n\n\t\"github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom/demo/go\"\n)\n\nfunc main() {\n\tn := maelstrom.NewNode()\n\n\t// A random token identifying this incarnation of the process, so that\n\t// tickets stay unique even if a node is ever restarted and its counter\n\t// reset.\n\tvar seed [8]byte\n\tif _, err := rand.Read(seed[:]); err != nil {\n\t\tpanic(err)\n\t}\n\tsession := hex.EncodeToString(seed[:])\n\n\tvar counter atomic.Uint64\n\n\tn.Handle(\"issue\", func(msg maelstrom.Message) error {\n\t\tticket := fmt.Sprintf(\"%s-%s-%d\", n.ID(), session, counter.Add(1))\n\t\treturn n.Reply(msg, map[string]any{\n\t\t\t\"type\":   \"issued\",\n\t\t\t\"ticket\": ticket,\n\t\t})\n\t})\n\n\tif err := n.Run(); err != nil {\n\t\tpanic(err)\n\t}\n}\n```\n\nWait: one issue — n.ID() may be empty if init hasn't happened? Messages only arrive after init, so it's fine.\n\nAnother consideration: the library's `n.Reply` handles in_reply_to automatically? Let's check: In maelstrom Go library, `Reply` sends a message to msg.Src with body including \"in_reply_to\": msg.Body[\"msg_id\"]. Yes, the library does that in Reply:\n\n```go\nfunc (n *Node) Reply(req Message, body any) error {\n\tbodyMap, ok := body.(map[string]any)\n\tif !ok {\n\t\t...\n\t}\n\t// If this is a request body, set in_reply_to.\n\tif to, ok := bodyMap[\"in_reply_to\"]; !ok {\n\t\t...\n\t}\n\t...\n}\n```\n\nLet me actually check the library source to be sure. Let me find the module cache path.\n\nLet me write the code and inspect the library source quickly.\n\nAlso, should I worry that the ticket needs to be exactly like \"n3-204\"? No, any JSON value.\n\nActually, hmm. One thought: maybe the graders also check that tickets look like the example? No — \"A ticket can be any JSON value: a string, a number, an array.\" So no format constraints.\n\nLet's write and build.\n\nOne more consideration about performance: 1000 req/s, library handles it fine.\n\nLet me also consider: could `--availability total` require that responses come within some latency? It checks that all requests are responded to (\"availability total\" means every request must be answered). Local reply always works.\n\nEdge: what if the rename wrapper requires the node to also handle `generate`? No, it renames to `issue`.\n\nLet me check the library API in the module cache.", "text": "", "tool_calls": [{"arguments": {"command": "ls $(go env GOMODCACHE)/github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom/ && ls $(go env GOMODCACHE)/github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom/demo/go@v0.0.0-20260710121306-480a8197026d/"}, "arguments_error": null, "id": "call_01a20414d5797b108b742a75", "name": "shell"}], "usage": {"input_tokens": 20040, "output_tokens": 943}}}
