set to n.id... In the output, the first message sent had no src, because n.id was empty at that point. Yes, `Send` uses n.id as src; since init hadn't completed, src was \"\". That confirms the race matters for Maelstrom protocol correctness too (dest/src must be set). So gating on init is important.\n\nActually wait — Maelstrom itself won't send client requests before init completes? In Maelstrom, `process` supervision: nodes receive init, and the nemesis/clients start after... I recall in Maelstrom the client requests can start right away, but the node process gets init first. The Go library's race exists but is usually benign because init is processed first in practice (goroutine scheduling order: the init goroutine is started first and likely runs first). But under load it could theoretically interleave. With the gate, it's safe.\n\nImplementation:\n\n```go\nready := make(chan struct{})\nvar nodeID atomic.Pointer[string]  // or use a mutex\nn.Handle(\"init\", func(msg maelstrom.Message) error {\n    // capture ID\n    id := n.ID()\n    ...\n    close(ready)\n    return nil\n})\n```\n\nBut careful: my \"init\" handler returns nil, and then handleInitMessage replies init_ok. Good. But if the init handler is never called (shouldn't happen), the issue handler would block forever → request never answered → availability failure. Add a fallback: wait on ready with a timeout (e.g., 5 seconds), then proceed with whatever ID we have. Hmm, but if init hasn't arrived, n.ID() is \"\" and src would be \"\" in replies → Maelstrom would flag it. Better: wait indefinitely? A request that never gets answered fails availability. A request answered with empty src fails protocol. Practically init always arrives first, so the gate will open immediately. I'll use a select with timeout of, say, 10 seconds as a safety valve, and use the ID if available; else empty. Actually, better fallback: if init hasn't arrived after timeout, still reply (availability matters more, and Maelstrom's requests come only after init anyway).\n\nHmm, wait. Is it possible Maelstrom sends an issue request before init_ok? Let me think about Maelstrom internals: In Maelstrom, each node process is started, and the \"init\" RPC is sent. Maelstrom's `net` layer... The client generator starts after the cluster is \"up\" I believe. Even if not, our gate handles it.\n\nActually there's a subtlety: if a request arrives before init and we hold it for a few ms until init completes, the response is delayed slightly — fine.\n\nBut careful about another thing: the library reads from stdin sequentially and dispatches; if init hasn't been read yet but issue was received... init is always the first line Maelstrom writes to a fresh process. So the gate will open within microseconds.\n\nLet me implement:\n\n```go\npackage main\n\nimport (\n\t\"crypto/rand\"\n\t\"encoding/hex\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"log\"\n\t\"sync\"\n\t\"sync/atomic\"\n\t\"time\"\n\n\tmaelstrom \"github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom/demo/go\"\n)\n\nvar (\n\treadyOnce sync.Once\n\tready     = make(chan struct{})\n\tnodeIDMu  sync.Mutex\n\tnodeID    string\n)\n\nfunc main() {\n\tn := maelstrom.NewNode()\n\t...\n}\n```\n\nSimpler: use a struct with atomic value + channel.\n\nLet me write clean code:\n\n```go\ntype node struct {\n\tn *maelstrom.Node\n\tready chan struct{}\n\treadyOnce sync.Once\n\tid atomic.Value // string\n}\n\nfunc (x *node) markReady() { x.readyOnce.Do(func(){ close(x.ready) }) }\n\nfunc (x *node) awaitReady(timeout time.Duration) {\n\tselect {\n\tcase <-x.ready:\n\tcase <-time.After(timeout):\n\t}\n}\n```\n\nThe init handler sets id and marks ready. The issue handler awaits ready (with, say, 30s timeout as safety valve—hmm, if init never arrives, the whole test fails anyway; use 5s).\n\nWait — actually there's another consideration: the counter. Should the counter be per-process? Yes.\n\nLet me also double check: does Maelstrom restart nodes during a partition nemesis? No, only network partitions. But some tests have