{"seq": 48, "type": "assistant_message", "ts": "2026-11-22T16:29:30.751109+00:00", "payload": {"reasoning": "", "text": "Done. `/work` contains `main.go`, `go.mod`/`go.sum`, and a freshly built `node` binary.\n\n**Design:** total availability plus global uniqueness rules out any coordination, so each node mints ticket numbers entirely locally. A ticket is the string `<nodeID>-<incarnation>-<counter>`, e.g. `n3-9f2c1a7b-204`:\n\n- **node ID** (`n1`…`n5`) — distinct per machine, so tickets from different machines never collide, even across partitions.\n- **incarnation** — 8 random bytes, so a restarted process whose counter starts over can't re-issue an old ticket.\n- **counter** — an atomic `uint64`, incremented once per request.\n\nRequests are answered with a single local `Reply`, so a node cut off from everyone still responds instantly.\n\nOne wrinkle I hit: the Maelstrom Go library runs each handler in its own goroutine, so a request can race with the `init` message and see an empty node ID (which also would leave `src` blank in replies). The `issue` handler now waits on a gate that is closed by an `init` handler (with a 5 s safety valve so a request can never hang).\n\n**Testing:** three runs of the exact graded command, all `:valid? true` — e.g. 27 955 / 27 999 / 27 992 requests attempted, all ok, `ok-fraction 1.0`, `duplicated-count 0`, \"Everything looks good!\". Build verified from a clean state with `go build -o node .`.", "tool_calls": [], "usage": {"input_tokens": 29611, "output_tokens": 348}}}
